::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L07 Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Scenario: A student is preparing a presentation about source evaluation. Which choice best shows a clear perspective and coherent focus? \{
~A. The presentation lists several unrelated school topics.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~B. The presentation uses a decorative image with no explanation.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=C. The presentation states one clear point about source evaluation and uses details that support that point.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~D. The presentation avoids stating what the audience should understand.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L07 Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Slide note: A student wants to support a presentation about revision clarity. Which slide note is most audience-ready? \{
~A. Lots of stuff about revision clarity and other things.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~B. A copied paragraph with every source detail on one slide.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. An unrelated sentence that does not support the message.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=D. Concise evidence connects revision clarity to the presentation perspective.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L07 Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.03
Speaker note: A slide gives a short point about peer feedback. Which speaker note best explains rather than repeats the slide? \{
=A. This point matters because it helps the audience understand how peer feedback supports the overall message.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~B. I will read the slide exactly as written.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. This slide is here because it is on the screen.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. Now I will talk about a different topic.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.03 U06 L07 Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Revision task: Which change would most improve clarity for a presentation about  \{
~A. Add more unrelated facts to make the slide longer.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=B. Replace vague wording with precise language that names the communication purpose.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~C. Remove the perspective so the audience can guess the point.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. Use smaller text so a paragraph fits on the slide.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L07 Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.03
Readiness check: Which final check best prepares a presentation about research evidence for an audience? \{
~A. Check only whether the slides have colors.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~B. Add new unrelated content at the end.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=C. Confirm that slides, speaker notes, evidence, and wording support the same audience-focused message.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~D. Ignore conventions and source reliability.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

